blocks=[
 {"type":"paragraph","text":"4.12 Post-test exploratory model diagnostics","style":"Heading2"},
 {"type":"paragraph","text":"These supplementary analyses were performed after the original evaluation to examine explanations already raised in the report. They used the fixed supplied snapshot and all 30 selected checkpoints, without retraining, representation selection or threshold refitting. New inference reproduced all 90,270 original raw session scores exactly across 60 model–test cells. Decomposed contributions reconstructed each score within 9.54 × 10⁻⁷, reflecting float32 summation order; the original score comparison itself had zero difference. The following results supplement the original performance evaluation."},
 {"type":"paragraph","text":"Session length alone exposed a strong construction-related distinction in the primary test (Table 4.17). Ranking shorter sessions as more anomalous gave AP 0.5000 and AUROC 0.95655, because both nonroutine classes contained five events while routine sessions contained at least six. Within the 378 five-event threat and unusual legitimate sessions, length gave AP and AUROC of 0.5000. Both length-score directions are reported because this is a post-test descriptive contrast, not a selected replacement detector. It quantifies a benchmark cue but does not establish that the LSTM used that cue or isolate the causal effect of length."},
 {"type":"table","caption":"Table 4.17: Post-test length-only descriptive ranking","rows":length_rows,"widths":[2.0,1.0,1.1,0.85,0.9]},
 {"type":"paragraph","text":"Note. Scores are the signed number of events in each complete session. Threats are positive; all eligible legitimate sessions are negative. The held-out nonroutine comparison contains 189 threats and 189 unusual legitimate sessions. The known test contains 378 threats and 267 unusual legitimate sessions. These descriptors were examined after testing and were neither calibrated nor used to change the original detector."},
 {"type":"paragraph","text":"The loss decomposition shows which reconstruction groups numerically account for the adopted scores (Table 4.18). In FedAvg with SecAgg+, Boolean reconstruction contributed 81.32–81.69% of mean held-out threat scores and 84.66–87.05% of mean held-out unusual-legitimate scores across the two conditions, compared with 2.06–2.26% for routine activity. In the known test, the Boolean share was 68.82–69.48% for unusual legitimate activity but 23.34–24.95% for threats. Thus, both nonroutine held-out classes incur large Boolean reconstruction error; a large score does not by itself distinguish misuse from a legitimate exception. These are shares of the existing loss, not causal input importance or a retrained ablation."},
 {"type":"table","caption":"Table 4.18: Post-test reconstruction-loss shares for FedAvg with SecAgg+","rows":group_rows,"widths":[1.85,0.65,1.7,1.65]},
 {"type":"paragraph","text":"Note. Cat, Bool and Time denote categorical, Boolean and temporal contributions. For each seed and class, each share is the group's mean additive contribution divided by the mean original raw score, then averaged across the same five seeds. Contributions incorporate the original valid-target and real-event masks. Rounding may prevent shares from summing to 100%. Complete five-seed results and sample SDs for A, B and C are retained in the supplementary diagnostic records."},
 {"type":"paragraph","text":"The new entity tables extend the original pooled overlap check to client-specific sets. Every test session used actors already present in the same client's training data, leaving no unseen-actor group for comparison. Sessions containing at least one patient absent from same-client training numbered 230 of 1,986 held-out routine sessions, 14 of 189 held-out threats, 14 of 189 held-out unusual legitimate sessions, 131 of 378 known threats and 75 of 267 known unusual legitimate sessions. These counts describe recurrence rather than memorisation: actor and patient identifiers were excluded from model inputs, and the observations are not entity-disjoint validation. Appendix C.9 reports the aggregate counts; full set sizes and within/between-client intersections are retained in the supplement."},
 {"type":"paragraph","text":"Target coverage and duplicated location fields were examined without changing model inputs. At fixed predictions, replacing valid-field renormalisation by a denominator of thirteen reduced C's mean class-level raw scores by approximately 0.15–1.57%. Omitting only the ward_or_location output term while averaging the remaining categorical terms increased those class means by approximately 0.39–5.77%, because that output had relatively low reconstruction error. This arithmetic result is not the effect of removing duplicated input information and retraining. The original categorical mask, representation, calibration and performance results remain unchanged. Controlled representation studies with fresh evaluation are still needed to test causal explanations."}
]
