appendix=[
 {"type":"paragraph","text":"C.9 Supplementary diagnostic details","style":"Heading2"},
 {"type":"paragraph","text":"The post-test diagnostics in Section 4.12 used unchanged checkpoints, encoded test inputs and validation thresholds. The supplement includes executable diagnostic code, input file identities, aggregate entity matrices, all model/condition/seed loss contributions, within-class/client score associations and sixteen anonymous illustrative records. It contains no raw event records, actor/patient identifiers or learned weights. Full client-specific intersections extend the earlier pooled descriptive record; they do not reconstruct the original partition-generation algorithm."},
 {"type":"table","caption":"Table C.10: Same-client training-entity recurrence in the fixed tests","rows":entity_rows,"widths":[1.85,0.6,1.4,1.7]},
 {"type":"paragraph","text":"Note. An actor is seen if its identifier appears in that client's training events. The patient column counts sessions containing at least one patient identifier absent from the same client's training events; it is not the number of new patients. Multiple sessions may concern the same entity. Actor recurrence is complete in all five groups, precluding an unseen-actor test within this snapshot."},
 {"type":"table","caption":"Table C.11: Fixed-output score-arithmetic checks for C","rows":arows,"widths":[1.85,0.6,1.55,1.55]},
 {"type":"paragraph","text":"Note. Values are the mean of five seed-specific percentage changes in the class mean raw loss relative to the original score. Ref and NI mean Reference and controlled Non-IID. Fixed-13 retains zero loss for excluded categorical targets but uses thirteen instead of the valid-field count as denominator. Omit ward-head removes only that output term and renormalises the remaining valid categorical fields. Inputs, weights and predictions are fixed; neither calculation is a newly calibrated detector or a causal input ablation."},
 {"type":"table","caption":"Table C.12: Anonymous illustrative held-out decisions for C, Reference, seed 11","rows":case_rows,"widths":[1.65,0.6,0.45,1.2,1.65]},
 {"type":"paragraph","text":"Note. Each row is the middle-scoring session within its source-label/unchanged-decision cell, selected using the original raw score. Anonymous ordinals refer to sorted test-input order, not actor, patient or session identifiers. TP, FP, FN and TN denote true positive, false positive, false negative and true negative under the supplied labels and original strict q = 0.99 rule. These four examples illustrate distinct score compositions; they are not a representative sample or an adjudication of clinical appropriateness."},
 {"type":"paragraph","text":"Associations were computed separately within each test, source class and client, then summarised across the same five training seeds. Constant predictors were left undefined. For example, length is constant within both five-event held-out nonroutine classes, so their length–score correlation cannot be estimated. Patient recurrence and exclusion rates are observational attributes that can vary with scenario and other inputs. Neither these correlations nor the additive loss shares identify a unique cause of the errors."}
]
